  "guide": {"focus":"The ball and floor have different bounciness, so the rule that combines their values sets the rebound height.","predict":{"prompt":"Which rule produces the highest first rebound?","options":["Bouncier surface","Average","Less bouncy surface"]},"variables":[{"control":"world.collisions.bounce_rule","label":"Bounce rule"}],"steps":[{"text":"Run once and compare the peak of the first bounce with the four height markers."},{"text":"Choose another bounce rule, press R, and repeat the drop.","control":"world.collisions.bounce_rule"},{"text":"Run Bounce rules vs theory in Theory checks.","open":"measure.theory.collisions_run"}],"expect":"Using the bouncier surface's value gives the highest rebound. Using the less bouncy surface's value gives the lowest. The peak of each first bounce should be close to its marker.","limits":"The markers predict the first bounce for an ideal vertical impact. Numerical and contact errors accumulate over later bounces."},
